-- Every entry's day, stored (TS-111, docs/decisions.md entry 39). Migration
-- 0020 added `time_entries.spent_on` empty; since TS-111b every writer gives
-- it. This fills it for the entries tracked before, then refuses any row
-- without a real date, and indexes it for the readers, which read it from
-- TS-111c on.
--
-- An entry tracked before is given the day its start falls on in its
-- person's zone as it is today: their own, else their organisation's, the
-- zone the Worker read its day in until now. That is the cost: an entry made
-- before a person last changed their zone takes the new zone's day, which can
-- differ from the day it was shown on at the time.
--
-- SQLite knows no time zones, so each zone's offsets from UTC, from 2000 to
-- 2040, are written below by `node scripts/entry-day-backfill.ts`, a statement
-- for each set of zones with the same offsets. An invoiced entry may have its
-- empty day written once (0020's trigger); nothing else of it changes. A zone
-- the script did not know is read as UTC by the last statement.
UPDATE `time_entries` SET `spent_on` = date(`started_at` / 1000 + 10800, 'unixepoch')
WHERE `spent_on` = '' AND coalesce((SELECT `time_zone` FROM `users` WHERE `users`.`id` = `time_entries`.`user_id`), (SELECT `time_zone` FROM `organisations` WHERE `organisations`.`id` = `time_entries`.`organisation_id`)) IN ('Africa/Addis_Ababa','Africa/Asmera','Africa/Dar_es_Salaam','Africa/Djibouti','Africa/Kampala','Africa/Mogadishu','Africa/Nairobi','Antarctica/Syowa','Asia/Aden','Asia/Bahrain','Asia/Kuwait','Asia/Qatar','Asia/Riyadh','Indian/Antananarivo','Indian/Comoro','Indian/Mayotte');
--> statement-breakpoint
